  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA beautiful Thanksgiving was being enjoyed at midday, with\nscattered cirrus and light winds. With the light winds and poor\nmixing, temperatures will stay several degrees below normal this\nafternoon, in the mid to upper 40s. \n\nCompared to last night, temperatures tonight and Friday morning\nare expected to be about 10 degrees milder, courtesy of gentle\nwesterly downslope overnight and warm advection aloft. \n\nMuch improved weather is expected Friday, with noticeably milder\ntemperatures in the lower 60s. Northwest downslope compression\nthrough the lower troposphere will allow temperatures to bounce\nback well above late November normals. \n\nFast moving shortwave is consistently forecast by models to\nenter the Pacific Northwest around 6 pm Friday, race through the\nRockies Saturday, and reach Kansas Saturday night. This\nshortwave will be much too progressive to produce significant\nrain/snow in southwest Kansas, but holiday travelers will want\nto be aware of snowfall accumulation potential across Nebraska,\nIowa and northeast Kansas. Increasing clouds are expected\nSaturday, but SW KS is expected to remain in the mild warm\nsector through about sunset/6 pm. Afternoon temperatures in \nthe 50s will prevail. Cold front will race through SW KS in\nabout 3 hours time Saturday evening, accompanied by intense\nnorth winds. Models project 850 mb winds, about 2k feet AGL, \nin the 50-70 mph range, Saturday evening. Gusts of that\nmagnitude are probable, when/where sufficient mixing can be\nachieved, especially along the preferred US 83 corridor. \nHigh wind watches/warnings may be required. Along with\nneighboring WFOs, increased wind grids Saturday evening \nto the 90%ile of the NBM.\n\nAt least scattered rain and/or snow showers are expected to\nfavor the northeast counties, northeast of Dodge City, Saturday\nevening. Any rain/snow amounts will be strongly limited in \nSW KS with the expected short residency time. Still, passing\nsnow squalls could limit visibility significantly along the \nI-70 corridor Saturday evening. Most models suggest thickness\nwill fall sufficiently fast before precipitation ends to support\na rain to snow phase change. \n\nSunday will be briefly cooler behind the departing system, back \nto normal in the 40s, but there is no cold/arctic air to tap\nbehind this system. Afternoon temperatures will show another \nrapid warming trend Monday through Wednesday, back through the \n50s. NBM reasonably projects 60s next Wednesday as SWly \ndownslope flow is established. High confidence that above normal\ntemperatures will continue through at least December 10th.",
